From 575190efa825c503dc156e4f8ea875c34638829b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 02:06:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09316/11362] Lower vLLM concurrency 24 -> 8: at 24 the MEDIAN
 induction exceeds the scored timeout

The old default was sized against memory and diminishing returns -- fp8 KV fits ~22
sessions, the curve bends by k=32 -- and both are still true. Neither is what binds.

What binds is the scored per-call timeout of 2400 s. Aggregate throughput at concurrency
is bought by LOWERING the per-stream rate, so more sessions means each induction takes
longer against a fixed ceiling, and a fired timeout does not slow the run, it loses that
induction: generate() fails and the agent proceeds with no world model for that call.

Tonight's goal-defect A/B supplied the number the analysis was missing -- nine real
Qwen3.8-27B induction lengths, 36406 to 83444 generated tokens, median 62490. Against
per-stream rates measured on the scored Blackwell card:

    k=8    40.0 tok/s   median 1562s ok    max 2086s ok     ~320 tok/s aggregate
    k=16   30.2         median 2069s ok    max 2763s OVER   ~483
    k=24   23.6         median 2648s OVER  max 3536s OVER   ~567   <- the old default
    k=32   20.4         median 3063s OVER  max 4090s OVER   ~652

At 24 the median exceeds the timeout, not merely the tail. 8 is the highest concurrency
where even the longest observed induction fits, and it still runs ~6.1x the llama.cpp
configuration it replaces. Trading 12.5x for 6.1x to move the median from over-budget to
inside is the right side of that bet.

Limits stated in the docstring rather than glossed: the draws came from a dev 3090 under
the A/B's think envelope rather than the scored one, the k=8 and k=24 per-stream rates are
interpolated between measured k=4/16/32, and the eval's real concurrency is the number of
games in flight. Raising this above 8 without re-measuring induction length is the
specific thing not to do.

